\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TVerbpattern9
\item[* relevant examples:] in general: IONP\_PREP..
\begin{description}
 \item[subrule 1a/b:] IONP\_PREPCLOSEDADJPPROP\\
  (vp012): ?strike x1 as x2: He struck me as pompous\\
  The fact that `me' is considered an indirect object here is caused by two 
things:\\
1) if the verb is really ergative, `he pompous' must be the direct object, so 
there is not much choice for `me'\\
2) the Dutch translation has `overkomen {\em op mij\/}', which looks much more 
like an indirect object.
 \item[subrule 2a/d:] IONP\_PREPNP\\
  (vp123): x1 ask/tell x2 about x3: Why don't you ask/tell him about it?\\
  (vp123): x1 provide x2 with x3: I provided them with the relevant documents
\end{description}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) english
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] Verbppropformation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class)TC: 
Verbpatternrules
\item[* date of creation:] 23-DEC-1987
\item[* author:] Margeet Sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RVerbpattern10, 11, 12
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 30/08/88\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders, jan odijk: SpecQ, 
aspect, class, deixis, retro. For comment, see Verbpattern 6.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] Checks if the category of the variables is compatible with the 
category of the arguments that the verb expects. The rule also introduces the 
preposition and the prep-phrase necessary for the prepositional object. Note 
that the object required first in the model is an indirect object or, at least, 
the object that is analysed last in a construction without preps. The prep has 
to go with the direct object in this model.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] each subrule 
handles a specific verbpattern
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
